Follow-up to results/bit/profile_instancenorm_splice_feasibility.log, which profiled the
real CPU cost of resnetv2_50x3_xint8.onnx's 49 InstanceNormalization nodes (VitisAI EP
has no NPU kernel for the op) and projected which of their 6 shapes a hand-written bf16
AIE kernel could beat. This log is that kernel, written and measured: kernels/
groupnorm_bf16/ (groupnorm.py = IRON design + harness, groupnorm_kernels.cc = the AIE
C++, extract_golden.py = pulls a real node's tensors out of the model). Same mlir-aie
v1.4.2 / ironenv / Peano setup as the other mlir_aie_*.log files here; no mlir-aie
example was modified or reused as a template -- ml/norm (the only norm design) is
npu2/Strix-only, so this is from scratch.

Order of work, per the user's call: write the largest shape (L=301056) first, because
it has the widest projected margin and the fewest nodes -- if it lands near the 1489us
projection the model is credible and the smaller shapes follow; if it lands far off,
that's learned in 3 nodes instead of 14.

--- What the kernel is ---

GroupNorm(32) over a [1, 32, L] bf16 tensor:  y[g,:] = scale[g]*(x[g,:]-mean[g])/
sqrt(var[g]+eps) + bias[g], eps = 1e-5 (the fp32 9.999999747378752e-06 every node in
the model carries). Layout on the npu1 array: 8 workers, two per column on rows 2-3 of
all four columns, each owning 4 consecutive groups (a contiguous 4*L block). Each worker
has its own input and output ObjectFifo pinned to its column's shim tile, so every one
of the device's 8 shim MM2S and 8 S2MM channels carries exactly one stream -- the point
of the layout, since the op is DMA-bound. Per worker the host queues 3 fills and 1
drain: a params object (that core's scale/bias as raw fp32 bits inside one bf16 chunk),
the 4*L block for pass 1 (statistics), the same block again for pass 2 (normalise),
and the 4*L output. The two-pass reduction therefore never touches the host. Compute:
pass 1 keeps 16-lane fp32 sum / sum-of-squares accumulators per group via the bf16 x
bf16 -> fp32 MAC path (products exact); between passes each group's (sum, sumsq,
scale, bias) folds to an affine pair (a, b); pass 2 is y = a*x + b as two MACs per
vector (a split into a bf16 hi part and bf16 residual so it keeps ~16 mantissa bits,
b seeding the fp32 accumulator exactly), rounded to bf16 round-to-nearest-even.

Two toolchain facts learned on the way, both now in the source comments:
  - Peano's AIE libc has no float sqrtf (compile error: "no matching function for
    call to 'sqrt'", only the integral template exists) -- the kernel uses a software
    reciprocal sqrt (bit-hack seed + 3 Newton steps, fp32-exact; 4 calls per core).
  - A shim tile has 2 DMA channels per direction, and two cores per column already use
    both, so the params can't ride a third fifo; they go down the data fifo as object 0.

--- Numerical check ---

Every run below compares the kernel output against (1) a float64 GroupNorm of the
bf16-rounded input the kernel actually sees (centred two-pass variance), (2) that same
reference rounded to bf16 the way the kernel's output is, and, for the golden runs,
(3) ORT's own CPU fp32 InstanceNormalization output for the real node on the real
image (data/eval/000000.jpg at 448x448, via extract_golden.py in resnet_env17). Pass
criterion: every element within 0.02 + |y|/256 of (1). In every run the "kernel vs
f64 reference" row is numerically identical to the "bf16-rounded reference vs f64
reference" row -- i.e. the kernel's entire error is the bf16 output quantisation
(rel-L2 ~0.17% per group), and against the bf16-rounded reference it is bit-exact
except for a handful of round-half cases (mean|err| <= 8e-6). The kernel's E[x^2]-mean^2
variance is safe on these tensors: per-group |mean|/std tops out at 0.72 (L=75264),
and its deviation from the centred value is at fp32 noise (<= 2.6e-15).

All golden nodes have scale = 1.0 and bias = 0.0 exactly: the exporter's GroupNorm ->
InstanceNormalization reshape trick applies the real per-channel affine as separate
Mul/Add nodes after the reshape back, so the InstanceNormalization node itself is
identity-affine. The kernel's affine path is still exercised by the random-data runs
(scale in [0.5, 1.5], bias in [-0.5, 0.5]).

--- Runs (Desktop 2 / Phoenix, ironenv, python groupnorm.py -d npu ...) ---

Timing: "NPU time" is mlir-aie's own bracket -- kernel dispatch through completion
(xrt run + wait), the same bracket behind the 56.19 GB/s memcpy figure in
mlir_aie_examples_npu.log -- and excludes host-side sync. "End-to-end" wraps the whole
Python call. 2 warmup + 10 timed iterations each. Chunk = ObjectFifo object size in
bf16 elements; every L below is chunk*49 except where noted.

