L=9408 was not run: its CPU cost (117.6us) is already below the ~185-200us fixed floor
measured by the overhead probe, so no configuration of this design can win there.

Compile artifacts (the two L=301056 builds): ~/.npu/cache/974a6150c19cb3252c784ebe
(chunk 3072, final.xclbin 60,408 B) and bea0980d1871a982eb8da426 (chunk 6144,
final.xclbin 71,421 B), each with 8 core ELFs elfs_main_core_{0,1,2,3}_{2,3} -- the
4-column x 2-row placement the design pins, all four columns' shim DMAs in use.

--- Verdict: measured against the feasibility log's projection and CPU cost ---

CPU us/call is the profiled figure from results/bit/profile_instancenorm_splice_
feasibility.log (ORT's own profiler, real inference, same model). NPU us/call is the
golden run's avg (min in parentheses).

  L         nodes  CPU us/call   projected   NPU us/call (min)     per call    x nodes
  9408        5       117.6         492      not run (floor ~200)  CPU wins    --
  18816      11       233.3         524      261.7  (254.8)        CPU +28us   --
  37632      11       495.8         589      351.0  (337.1)        NPU +145us  +1.59 ms
  75264      14       989.4         717      510.1  (501.9)        NPU +479us  +6.71 ms
  150528      5      1899.3         974      835.8  (807.7)        NPU +1063us +5.32 ms
  301056      3      3472.1        1489     1535.2 (1481.6)        NPU +1937us +5.81 ms

  - The 301056 row landed on its projection (1535 vs 1489us avg; the min, 1482, is
    within 0.5%). The projection's two halves were both wrong in ways that cancelled:
    the fixed overhead is ~200us not 460, and the traffic moves at 37-39 GB/s in this
    design rather than memcpy's 56 GB/s (pass 1 is read-only, so half the time only
    one DMA direction is busy; memcpy's figure was full duplex).
  - Everything below it beat its projection, because the real overhead is smaller
    than assumed. That flips the 37632 row: "CPU wins (barely)" in the projection,
    a measured 145us/call kernel win here -- narrow, but on 11 nodes.
  - 18816 and 9408 stay on CPU, as projected; 18816 loses by only 28us/call.
  - Total across the 33 convertible nodes (4 shapes): ~19.4ms of the op's 42.37ms per
    inference, i.e. InstanceNorm's cost would drop to ~23ms -- against the feasibility
    log's ~28ms projection, and its "22 nodes" is now 33.

What this does NOT show: (a) the model's end-to-end latency with the kernel spliced in.
The two-process pipeline (VitisAI EP session in resnet_env17 handing tensors to the
ironenv process, shown viable at the hardware level in the feasibility log) is not
built; its handoff cost is unmeasured and will decide how much of the per-call margin
survives -- the harness's own End-to-end minus NPU-time gap (~0.42-0.50ms per call
here, Python-side dispatch plus buffer handling) is the scale of host-side cost to
beat, and by itself it would erase the 37632 win and most of the 75264 one if it
carried over unchanged. (b) Accuracy of the full model with bf16 in place of fp32 for
these nodes: per-node error is the bf16 output rounding floor (rel-L2 ~0.17%), but the
top-1 effect of 33 such nodes in series has not been measured. Both are the next
measurements, not assumptions.

Machine: Desktop 2 / Phoenix. Kernel runs in the mlir-aie ironenv; golden extraction in
resnet_env17 (CPU EP only, no NPU contention with the timed runs).
